=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Ticket #8155 claims router16 is dead and Campus 1 (172.16.10.x / 20.x / 30.x) is unreachable, but every layer of live measurement contradicts this.

**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are healthy.

**Mechanism check:** Router16 is a bonded-uplink aggregation router sitting between mswitch0 (the Campus 1 multilayer switch) and router7 (the WAN core). For the reported symptom to be real, router16's FRR/OSPF would need to be dead, or its bond0 or eth2 link would need to be down. Neither is true: ospf_snapshot confirms router16 has FRR daemons active, vtysh reachable, bond0 (10.2.2.1/24 → mswitch0) and eth2 (172.30.1.1/24 → router7) both in OSPF Full state with one adjacency each.

**Localization:** mswitch0 redistributes 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, and 172.16.30.0/24 into OSPF area 0.0.0.0. All 9 routers carry 17 OSPF routes each, indicating the Campus 1 prefixes are fully propagated. End-to-end pings from outside hosts to Campus 1 PCs (e.g., pc1→pc14, dc_pc5→pc14, pc9→pc14) return 0% loss.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The network is operating normally. Router16 is alive, its OSPF adjacencies are up, and Campus 1 traffic flows without loss.

**Fix:** No corrective action needed. Advise the helpdesk that the reported outage cannot be reproduced — Campus 1 connectivity is fully functional at time of investigation. If the issue was transient, it has self-resolved prior to this diagnostic run; no evidence of a prior mechanism (flap script, carrier disruption, OSPF instability) was found.
=== END ===